The child is probably re-rendering because you're creating new objects or functions in the parent's render method—even though they have the same value, they have different memory addresses.

For example:

```jsx
// ❌ creates a new object every render
<Child data={{ name: "Alice" }} />

// ❌ creates a new function every render
<Child onClick={() => console.log("click")} />
```

React compares props by reference (`===`), not by value. So even though `{ name: "Alice" }` looks the same, it's a fresh object each time.

**To fix it:**

Move object and function creation outside the render:

```jsx
const data = { name: "Alice" };
const handleClick = () => console.log("click");

export function Parent() {
  return <Child data={data} onClick={handleClick} />;
}
```

Or use `useMemo` and `useCallback` to keep the same reference:

```jsx
export function Parent() {
  const data = useMemo(() => ({ name: "Alice" }), []);
  const handleClick = useCallback(() => console.log("click"), []);
  return <Child data={data} onClick={handleClick} />;
}
```

Then wrap your child in `React.memo` to skip rendering when props haven't changed:

```jsx
export const Child = React.memo(({ data, onClick }) => {
  return <div>{data.name}</div>;
});
```

The memo + stable props combo stops the re-render.
